// ─── i18n for setup-guide.html ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The guide's own strings, merged into TRANSLATIONS (translations.js) so the
// shared t() / applyI18n() / setLanguage() machinery drives them. They live in
// their own file because only the guide needs them — the app would otherwise
// download ~250 guide strings it never shows.
//
// The ENGLISH text of the page is NOT here: it stays in the markup, and the
// guide's script copies each tagged element's English into TRANSLATIONS.en
// before the first translation pass. So an English edit is made in the HTML
// only — but the same key here must then be re-translated in all three
// languages, or ES / PT readers keep the old meaning.
// Only the strings the guide's script builds (wizard buttons, progress, page
// title) have English here, because they have no markup to be read from.
//
// Naming rules the translations follow, so the guide matches what people see:
//  • Buttons in dankcharts' Settings window stay in ENGLISH (Save & Load,
//    Sheet URL or ID, Use custom API credentials, Connect to Last.fm, …) —
//    that window is not translated, so a translated name would not be found.
//  • Labels the app DOES translate use the app's own wording (Configurar,
//    Dados Brutos, Enviar a Last.fm, ✚ Agregar …) — see translations.js.
//  • Menu items the Apps Script adds to the sheet ("Last.fm → Start Auto-Update
//    (90 min)", "Fetch Latest Tracks") and the template's "Settings" tab stay
//    English — the script and the template are English.
//  • Google, Spotify, Apple and Deezer screens use those products' own
//    localized wording for that language, since that is what the reader sees.
// Placeholders use the {{name}} syntax t() substitutes.

